---
/**
 * /commercial-stucco/milestone-inspection-repairs/ — commercial child page.
 * keyword-plan architecture :264 (marked blue ocean), sequencing :412 Wk 9-10.
 * Brief: campaigns/content-plan/milestone-inspection-stucco-repairs.md
 *
 * Target: `milestone inspection stucco repair contractor florida`.
 * Secondary: `phase 2 report exterior wall repair`, `condo milestone
 * inspection repairs volusia`, `who repairs stucco after milestone
 * inspection`, `repairs must begin 365 days phase 2 report`.
 *
 * "PHASE 2" IS NO LONGER THE PAGE'S MARKETING LANGUAGE (changed 2026-09-02).
 * The H1 used to read "Phase 2 flagged your exterior walls," the primary CTA
 * said "Send us the Phase 2 report," and the term ran through the hero, the
 * open-wall section heads and the CTA. Two problems with that. First, it is
 * the vocabulary of the engineer's document rather than of the buyer's
 * situation, and it made the whole page speak only to a board that already
 * held a phase two report — excluding every association that is budgeting,
 * building a reserve line, or simply approaching its inspection date, which
 * is a larger group and an earlier, cheaper conversation. Second, leading
 * with a statutory stage name reads as jargon-forward rather than
 * procurement-competent.
 *
 * "phase one" and "phase two" are the STATUTE'S OWN TERMS (FS 553.899:
 * "A phase two of the milestone inspection must be performed if any
 * substantial structural deterioration is identified during phase one"), so
 * they are kept — and must be kept — everywhere they carry actual
 * information: the statute block explaining the process, the boundary
 * stating that we do not write those reports, and the FAQs, where they also
 * match how boards search. They were removed only from the headline, the
 * CTA, and section headings, where the plainer description of the same fact
 * ("invasive testing", "the engineer's report") is accurate, is not jargon,
 * and does not presume the reader's stage. Do NOT restore Phase 2 to the H1
 * or the CTA; do NOT strip it from the statute section, boundaries or FAQs.
 *
 * Angle ④ DEADLINE WORK. This buyer is not shopping craftsmanship or price.
 * They are managing liability and a date. Tone is procurement-competent:
 * specific, documented, scheduled. Not reassuring — reassurance is the
 * homeowner register and it reads as evasive to a board.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE GAP, RE-VERIFIED LIVE 2026-09-01 (`milestone inspection stucco repair
 * contractor Florida exterior wall repairs after Phase 2`): eight results,
 * all engineers (m2e, CSI, Florida Milestone Inspection LLC), inspection
 * firms (Team Complete, Building Mavens), and property managers (Campbell).
 * ZERO REPAIR CONTRACTORS. This is the strongest gap in the entire keyword
 * plan and it is still open. Every result explains the requirement; not one
 * is the trade that performs the work. The board reads five articles telling
 * them they have a deadline and none telling them who fixes it.
 *
 * THE HOOK THAT RESEARCH HANDED US, and the thing no competitor says:
 * Phase 2 testing ITSELF removes stucco and cladding to expose the structure
 * (sounding surveys, half-cell potential, material testing). So a building
 * that has been through Phase 2 has open wall — cut by the engineer, on
 * purpose, and left that way. Somebody has to close it back up, and that is
 * a stucco scope that exists on every Phase 2 building whether or not the
 * report names further wall repair. Not one page on this SERP mentions it.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * STATUTORY ACCURACY — RE-VERIFIED LIVE 2026-09-01, AND TWO CORRECTIONS.
 *
 * FS 553.899: buildings 3+ habitable stories; milestone inspection due by
 * December 31 of the year the building reaches 30 years of age. Phase two
 * follows a phase one finding of substantial structural deterioration;
 * required repairs "must be commenced within 365 days after receiving such
 * report" unless the local building department requires earlier.
 *
 * CORRECTION 2, MADE 2026-09-02 — THE 25-YEAR COASTAL TRIGGER IS NOT
 * AUTOMATIC. This page previously stated the inspection is due "at 30 years,
 * or at 25 years for buildings within three miles of the coast," and then
 * asserted that the coastal trigger "catches a great deal of the oceanfront
 * and Halifax River inventory five years earlier." BOTH WERE WRONG.
 *
 * Verified against the statute text at flsenate.gov (2024 and 2025): the
 * 30-year trigger is mandatory statewide, but the 25-year one is a LOCAL
 * OPTION — "the local enforcement agency MAY determine that local
 * circumstances, including environmental conditions such as proximity to
 * salt water... require" the earlier date. Whether Volusia County or any
 * municipality within it has adopted it is NOT VERIFIED, and the Spectrum
 * News 13 piece the local figures come from does not state that it has.
 * Many secondary sources (management companies, engineering firms) restate
 * the 25-year rule as if it were automatic; the primary source governs and
 * they are wrong.
 *
 * This mattered in the most damaging possible direction. The old copy told
 * boards their deadline was five years SOONER than the statute alone
 * requires. The page's entire value is deadline accuracy, so a board that
 * checks with its building department and finds the claim overstated
 * discards everything else on the page with it. The statute cell now states
 * the 30-year rule as law, presents the 25-year rule as the local option it
 * is, and routes the reader to their building department for the answer.
 * DO NOT restore the coastal trigger as an automatic rule, and do not assert
 * it applies anywhere in this service area without an adopted ordinance to
 * cite.
 *
 * FS 718.112(2)(g) SIRS: the brief and the hub both said "funding plans due
 * December 31, 2026." THAT WAS IMPRECISE AND IS CORRECTED HERE AND ON THE
 * HUB. HB 913 (signed 2025-06-22) moved the GENERAL deadline to
 * December 31, 2025 — already past. December 31, 2026 is a NARROW
 * CONDITIONAL OUTER LIMIT: an association whose milestone inspection is due
 * on or before that date may complete the SIRS alongside it, but no later.
 * Shipping the loose version would have told most boards they had a year
 * they do not have. On a page selling to people managing a legal deadline,
 * getting the deadline wrong is the one unrecoverable error.
 *
 * LOCAL SCALE ADDED 2026-09-01, FROM THE VOLUSIA LOCATION RESEARCH. The
 * location-page splitting tests (keyword-plan, five cities, all rejected)
 * surfaced one genuinely strong fact that did not belong on a homeowner
 * location page: 86 condo buildings across 52 Volusia locations due for
 * milestone inspections, Ormond Beach 31 and New Smyrna Beach 21 (Spectrum
 * News 13, 2024-03-28, from county figures). That is a commercial/condo fact,
 * and this is the commercial page, so it landed here instead of justifying a
 * new URL. It is framed as SCHEDULING PRESSURE — the queue a board is
 * competing in — not as a claim about our work. The note under the statute
 * block states outright that we have performed no milestone work on any
 * building in that reporting. Do not convert this into proof, a project
 * count, or a service-area claim.
 *
 * STATUTE IS ATTRIBUTED, NEVER ADVISED. Public law cited as public law.
 * This page does not tell an association what its obligations are, does not
 * interpret its documents, and says so explicitly. We are a contractor.
 *
 * MILESTONE INSPECTIONS ARE NOT ABOUT STUCCO. They assess STRUCTURAL
 * integrity and explicitly set aside cosmetic cladding cracking. The page
 * must never imply we are part of the inspection or that cracked stucco is
 * what a milestone inspection looks for. Cladding is removed to REACH the
 * structure. We are downstream of the engineer, and the page is built on
 * that relationship rather than obscuring it.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * VERIFICATION. Rests on the same three flags as the hub (site.ts:329,341,
 * 358): commercialAndCondoWork, occupiedBuildingWork, multiStoryWork. The
 * third is load-bearing here specifically — a milestone building is 3+
 * habitable stories BY DEFINITION, so this page could not exist without it.
 *
 * NOT AUTHORIZED, and both were stated explicitly by the owner:
 *   - INSURANCE LIMITS. "Licensed and insured for commercial and condo
 *     work" is the whole sayable fact. No GL figure, aggregate,
 *     per-occurrence, WC detail, additional-insured or waiver-of-subrogation
 *     language, no certificate detail. The brief's `[GL LIMIT]` and
 *     `[WC COVERAGE]` slots are ROUTED TO A CONVERSATION, not stubbed.
 *   - ACCESS EQUIPMENT AND METHODS. No lift, boom, scissor, swing stage,
 *     scaffold, rope access, mast climber, or storey ceiling. The brief's
 *     occupied-building H2 asks for "staging, elevator use, noise windows" —
 *     NONE of that is verified. The section ships covering only what the
 *     flag actually authorizes: that we work occupied buildings and
 *     coordinate access with the association. The how is omitted.
 *
 * SECTIONS FROM THE BRIEF'S OUTLINE THAT ARE DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT:
 *   - "Projects we've completed" — requires [N COMPLETED MULTI-FAMILY
 *     PROJECTS] + [SQ FT / UNIT COUNTS]. Both null. A projects section with
 *     no projects is worse than no section. CUT ENTIRELY, not stubbed.
 *   - "References" — [PROPERTY MANAGER / BOARD REFERENCES] null. Cut.
 *   - "Documentation the board will want" — [SAMPLE DOCUMENTATION PACKAGE]
 *     null. We have never verified what we produce, so we cannot describe
 *     a package. Reframed to what the BOARD should require of ANY bidder,
 *     which is true, useful, and claims nothing about us.
 *   - [ENGINEER / CONSULTANT RELATIONSHIPS] — null. The page states we work
 *     downstream of the engineer, which is how the statute works, and does
 *     NOT claim relationships with any.
 *   - "Downloadable one-page capability sheet" from the brief's CTA — does
 *     not exist and cannot be honestly assembled from null proof fields.
 *     Omitted.
 *
 * SIRS ABSORBED HERE, NOT GIVEN ITS OWN URL. The architecture listed
 * /commercial-stucco/sirs-exterior-wall-reserves/ at keyword-plan:265; it
 * FAILS the splitting test — same buyer, same statute, same deadline,
 * differing only in which subsection of Ch. 718 applies. Building both
 * splits one small time-boxed keyword group across two thin URLs. SIRS
 * ships as an H2 plus FAQs on this page. Recorded in brand/assets.md.
 *
 * FIREWALL. Links UP to /commercial-stucco/ and out to /contact/. NOTHING
 * ELSE. No /stucco-repair/, no /problems/*, no /stucco-crack-triage/, no
 * /stucco-moisture-testing/ (that is a paid RESIDENTIAL product and routing
 * a board into it would be selling the wrong service). No homeowner page
 * links here. /commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/ was BUILT
 * 2026-09-01 and is now cross-linked from the funding FAQ, where a board
 * asking who pays for milestone repairs should be sent. Pillar complete.
 *
 * NO PRICE. `repairPriceRanges` and `pricingDisclaimer` deliberately not
 * imported — every range is a single-family repair and would misstate the
 * scale of a multi-elevation building by an order of magnitude.
 *
 * NOT AN INSPECTION. `concreteSpallingRepair: false` and
 * `standaloneWaterproofing: false` are acute risks HERE above anywhere else
 * on the site: spalling concrete is the single most common milestone
 * finding, and SIRS names waterproofing as a required component. A board
 * landing here will assume we do both. Named and routed out explicitly.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import MobileStickyBar from '../../../components/MobileStickyBar.astro';
import PageHero from '../../../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import FaqList from '../../../components/FaqList.astro';
import { business, serviceArea, email } from '../../../config/site';

const title =
  'Milestone Inspection Stucco & Exterior Wall Repairs | Volusia County';
const description =
  'Exterior wall repairs for Florida condominium and cooperative associations after a milestone inspection. We turn the engineer’s repair specification into a stucco scope, a number, and a schedule that holds your deadline.';

/**
 * What the statute actually says. Every line here was re-verified live
 * 2026-09-01. Attribution is in the markup, not implied.
 */
const statute = [
  {
    point: 'Which buildings',
    body: 'Condominium and cooperative buildings three or more habitable stories tall. The statute sets the milestone inspection at 30 years of age. It also allows the local enforcement agency to determine that local conditions — proximity to salt water among them — warrant requiring it at 25 years instead. Whether that earlier trigger has been adopted where your building sits is a question for your local building department, and it is worth asking early, because the answer moves your deadline by five years.',
  },
  {
    point: 'How many buildings this is, here',
    body: 'When Volusia County officials publicised the requirement, 86 condominium buildings across 52 locations in the county were identified as due for milestone inspections. Ormond Beach held the largest share at 31 locations, New Smyrna Beach the next at 21. That is a useful number for a board to have, because it means the engineers, the trades and the permitting counters are all working the same queue at the same time, and a scope that goes out to bid late competes with everyone else who also went out late.',
  },
  {
    point: 'Phase 1, then Phase 2 if warranted',
    body: 'Phase 1 is a visual inspection by a licensed engineer or architect. If it identifies substantial structural deterioration, Phase 2 follows. Phase 2 is invasive: sounding surveys for delaminated concrete, material testing, and removal of finishes and cladding to expose the structure underneath.',
  },
  {
    point: 'The 365-day clock',
    body: 'Where a Phase 2 report identifies required repairs, those repairs must begin within 365 days of the report unless the local building department requires an earlier start. That is the date most boards are actually working against, and it runs from the report rather than from the day the association decides to act.',
  },
  {
    point: 'The engineer writes the repair specification',
    body: 'Structural repair specifications and construction drawings are produced by a licensed professional engineer, and the association bids that specification out to contractors. Structural repair work requires permits. The design engineer typically observes construction and issues a final report confirming the deficiencies were addressed.',
  },
];

/**
 * THE PAGE'S BEST IDEA. No competitor mentions it and it is true of every
 * Phase 2 building. Ties directly to a stucco scope without a single
 * unverified claim.
 */
const openWall = [
  {
    h: 'Invasive testing leaves the wall open on purpose',
    body: 'To reach the structure, the invasive phase of a milestone inspection removes stucco and cladding at test locations. That is the point of it. When the engineer is finished, the building has openings that were cut deliberately and are not going to close themselves, and the report is about what was found rather than about restoring the finish.',
  },
  {
    h: 'Closing it back up is a stucco scope',
    body: 'Those openings need patching back into the surrounding finish, matched in texture and profile, and made weather-tight again. It exists on every building whose inspection reached the invasive stage, whether or not the report names any further exterior wall repair. Boards regularly overlook it because the engineer did not price it and it is not what the deadline is about.',
  },
  {
    h: 'And it is usually not the whole job',
    body: 'Once cladding comes off, what was behind it becomes visible for the first time in decades. If the engineer found water in the assembly, corroded lath, or failed flashing at the openings, the wall repair extends beyond the test patches. It is far cheaper to establish that while the wall is already open than to price it, close it, and reopen it later.',
  },
];

const boardChecks = [
  'The scope names specific elevations and approximate areas, not "the building."',
  'It states what happens when damage is found behind the finish — how it is documented, who approves it, and how it is priced — before anyone starts.',
  'It says whether the engineer’s test openings are included, because they are frequently missed by every bidder at once.',
  'It separates structural repair, which follows the engineer’s specification, from finish and cladding work, which is our scope.',
  'It states whether paint is included. This single line accounts for a large share of the spread between quotes.',
  'It says how work is staged around residents and what notice they receive.',
  'Every bidder is pricing the same written document. Until that is true, the quotes are not comparable to each other.',
];

const boundaries = [
  {
    label: 'We do not perform milestone inspections',
    body: 'Phase 1 and Phase 2 must be performed by a licensed engineer or architect, and the repair specification for structural work comes from a PE. We are not that, we do not write those reports, and we cannot tell you whether your building complies. We are the trade that performs the exterior wall scope afterwards.',
  },
  {
    label: 'We do not do concrete spalling or structural repair',
    body: 'Rusting rebar, spalling balcony edges, delaminated slab edges, and failing stair landings are outside our work. This matters more on this page than anywhere else on this site, because spalling concrete is one of the most common milestone findings and it sits on the same elevations as stucco damage. If your engineer’s report is largely about concrete, the contractor you need first is a concrete restoration contractor, not us.',
  },
  {
    label: 'We do not sell building-envelope waterproofing on its own',
    body: 'A structural integrity reserve study names waterproofing among its required components, so boards frequently arrive expecting us to bid it. Waterproofing may form part of a wall repair we perform, but we do not take it as a standalone contract. If that is the scope being procured, it needs a waterproofing contractor.',
  },
  {
    label: 'This page is not legal advice',
    body: 'FS 553.899 and FS 718.112 are public law and are cited here as public law. Deadlines, extensions, and how any of it applies to a specific building and its governing documents are questions for the association’s attorney and its engineer. Do not make a compliance decision on the strength of a contractor’s website.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Who repairs stucco after a milestone inspection?',
    a: 'A stucco or exterior wall contractor, working from the repair specification the engineer produced. The inspection identifies the deterioration and a licensed engineer specifies the repair; the association then bids that specification out. We perform the stucco and exterior wall portion of that work, including patching the openings the Phase 2 investigation cut into the cladding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How soon must repairs begin after a Phase 2 report?',
    a: 'Under FS 553.899, required repairs must begin within 365 days of receiving the Phase 2 report, unless the local building department requires an earlier start. In practice that time goes quickly, because the engineer has to produce the repair specification, the association has to bid it, fund it, and award it, and permits have to be issued before anyone starts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a milestone inspection look at cracked stucco?',
    a: 'Not on its own terms. A milestone inspection assesses structural integrity and explicitly sets aside cosmetic issues such as hairline cracking and paint condition. Cladding enters the picture because it has to be removed to reach the structure, or because the deterioration behind it involves water moving through the wall assembly. If your report names exterior walls, it is not describing a cosmetic problem.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Our Phase 2 left holes in the stucco. Is that included in the repair scope?',
    a: 'Often not, and it is worth checking. The engineer’s test openings are made to investigate, not to be restored, and the repair specification addresses the structural deficiencies that were found. Closing the openings back into matched finish is a separate scope that boards frequently discover has been left out of every quote they received. Ask each bidder directly whether their number includes it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a SIRS and does it cover exterior walls?',
    a: 'A Structural Integrity Reserve Study is a reserve study covering specified structural components, exterior painting and waterproofing among them, for buildings of three or more habitable stories under FS 718.112(2)(g). The general completion deadline was December 31, 2025. An association whose milestone inspection is due on or before December 31, 2026 may complete the study alongside that inspection, but no later than that date. Since HB 913 took effect, reserves for the required structural components can no longer be waived or reduced by an owner vote.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you give our reserve specialist a number for exterior wall work?',
    a: 'We can scope and price the exterior wall portion after looking at the building. We are not reserve specialists and do not prepare the study, but a reserve study is only as good as the costs in it, and an exterior wall line carried as a placeholder is the one most likely to be badly wrong on a coastal building.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you work while residents stay in the building?',
    a: 'Yes. We perform work on occupied buildings and coordinate access with the association or property manager — which elevations are worked when, how residents are notified, and what needs to be clear before work starts on a given face of the building. What that looks like specifically depends on the building and is settled with the manager rather than assumed in advance.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are you licensed and insured for condominium work?',
    a: 'Yes, we are licensed and insured for commercial and condominium work. Specific limits, certificate holders, and any additional-insured or endorsement requirements are handled directly with the association or its manager, because those requirements differ from building to building and most associations have their own. Ask us and we will provide current documentation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who pays for milestone repairs — the association or the owners?',
    a: 'Exterior walls and the building envelope are normally common elements, which makes their repair a common expense of the association rather than a bill to individual owners. Balconies are frequently limited common elements where the declaration may say otherwise, so a single elevation can carry more than one answer. How the association funds it — reserves, a special assessment, or borrowing — is a separate decision with its own notice requirements, and it has to be made inside the 365-day window.',
    href: '/commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/',
    hrefLabel: 'Who pays, and what a board needs before noticing an assessment',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do our exterior wall quotes vary so much?',
    a: 'Usually because they are not for the same work. Quotes diverge on whether repairing what is found behind the finish is included, whether all elevations are covered or only the worst one, whether the engineer’s test openings are in, whether preparation and finish matching are included, and whether paint is in or out. The spread is describing scope rather than value, and the lowest number generally belongs to the narrowest scope.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Milestone inspection stucco and exterior wall repairs',
  serviceType: 'Commercial stucco and exterior wall repair',
  provider: { '@type': 'LocalBusiness', name: business.name },
  ...(serviceArea ? { areaServed: serviceArea.map((a) => ({ '@type': 'Place', name: a })) } : {}),
  description:
    'Stucco and exterior wall repair for Florida condominium associations following a milestone inspection Phase 2 report, performed to the engineer’s repair specification.',
};

/**
 * BREADCRUMB SCHEMA IS NO LONGER BUILT HERE. Breadcrumbs.astro — rendered by
 * PageHero from the `crumbs` prop — emits its own BreadcrumbList JSON-LD and
 * prepends the Home entry this hand-rolled version was missing. Two
 * BreadcrumbList blocks on one page is a markup error, so the local one was
 * removed rather than kept alongside it. The rendered trail is unchanged
 * except that Home is now correctly present.
 */
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <SiteHeader current="/commercial-stucco/milestone-inspection-repairs/" />

  <main id="main">
    {/*
      HERO. No aside - the aside slot is where interior money pages put
      <ContactCta variant="inline" />, the homeowner component, which is not
      used anywhere in this cluster. h1 and lede are the exact strings the old
      local hero rendered. Breadcrumbs now come from PageHero and carry the
      same two-step trail, with Home correctly prepended.
    */}
    <PageHero
      crumbs={[
        { href: '/commercial-stucco/', label: 'Commercial & condo stucco' },
        { href: '/commercial-stucco/milestone-inspection-repairs/', label: 'Milestone inspection repairs' },
      ]}
      eyebrow="Milestone inspection repairs · FS 553.899"
      title="Exterior wall repairs for a milestone inspection. A scope, a number, and a schedule."
      lede="Whether your inspection is still ahead of you or the engineer’s report is already on the table, the exterior wall work has to be scoped, funded, bid, and started against a fixed date. Search that deadline and you will find engineers, inspection firms, management companies, and law-firm bulletins — every one of them explaining the requirement. Almost none of them is the trade that performs the repair. We are. This is what the exterior wall portion of a milestone repair program actually involves, and how to buy it without paying for it twice."
      actions={[
        { href: '#send', label: 'Send us the engineer’s report' },
        { href: '/commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/', label: 'Who pays for it', variant: 'secondary' },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            We perform commercial and condominium stucco work and are licensed and
            insured for it, including multi-story buildings and buildings that stay
            occupied while the work runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Boards reach this page at two different points. Some have a report in
            hand naming the exterior walls and a date to work back from. Others are
            planning ahead &mdash; budgeting, building a reserve line, or trying to
            understand what the exterior wall portion is likely to involve before
            the inspection happens. Both are worth a conversation, and the earlier
            one is generally the cheaper one, because scope decided under a deadline
            is scope decided at someone else&rsquo;s pace.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="The statute"
          title="What the statute actually requires"
          lede="Cited from public law, not offered as advice. Whether and how any of it applies to your building is a question for your engineer and your association's attorney."
        />

        <div class="grid-hairline cols-2">
          {statute.map((s) => (
            <div class="entry">
              <h3>{s.point}</h3>
              <p>{s.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <p class="note">
          Sources: FS 553.899 (milestone structural inspections) and FS
          718.112(2)(g) (structural integrity reserve studies), as amended.
          Building counts as reported by Spectrum News 13, March 2024, from
          Volusia County figures. We have not performed milestone-related work
          on any building named in that reporting, and we cite the count only
          because the scheduling pressure it describes is real.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* THE HOOK — the line item no competitor names. */}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="The missed line item"
          title="The open wall nobody quotes for"
          lede="This is the most commonly missed line item in a milestone repair program, and it exists on every building whose inspection reached the invasive stage."
        />

        <div class="grid-hairline cols-2">
          {openWall.map((o) => (
            <div class="entry">
              <h3>{o.h}</h3>
              <p>{o.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Report to scope"
          title="From the engineer's report to a scope you can bid"
        />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Your engineer wrote the report. Turning it into exterior wall work that
            can be bid, funded, and defended to owners is a separate job, and the
            365-day clock is already running while it happens. Bring us the report
            and we will tell you what the stucco and exterior wall scope inside it
            involves, what we would need to see on the building to price it, and
            what is missing from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The structural repairs follow the engineer&rsquo;s specification and
            are not ours to reinterpret. What we can establish is the wall scope
            that sits around them: which elevations, how much of the damage is
            finish versus water in the assembly, how the test openings and repair
            areas are closed back into matched finish, and what has to be staged
            around residents.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Procurement"
          title="What to require from every bidder"
          lede="This applies to us as much as to anyone else you ask. A board that holds all bidders to the list below is comparing prices. A board that does not is comparing documents that describe different jobs."
        />

        <ul class="checks prose">
          {boardChecks.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Access &amp; scheduling"
          title="Working an occupied building"
        />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Nearly all condominium exterior wall work happens with residents in
            place. We work occupied buildings and coordinate access with the
            association or property manager rather than leaving it to be resolved
            on the first morning &mdash; which elevations are worked when, how
            residents are notified, and what has to be clear before work starts on
            a given face of the building.
          </p>

          <p>
            How a specific building gets accessed depends on the building: its
            height, the elevations involved, what sits at ground level around it,
            and what the association permits on site. That is established when we
            look at the property. We would rather tell you it depends than tell
            you something confident and wrong before we have seen the building.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* COST. No number renders; `pricingDisclaimer` is not imported. */}
    <section class="section" id="cost">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead eyebrow="Cost" title="What this costs" />

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            We publish repair price ranges for single-property work elsewhere on
            this site and are deliberately not repeating them here. They describe
            a house. A multi-elevation building is not a large version of a
            residential repair, and a number derived that way would be wrong by
            an order of magnitude in either direction.
          </p>

          <p>
            This work is priced against a written scope after the building has
            been looked at and the Phase 2 report has been read. What moves the
            number is how many elevations are involved, how much of the damage is
            surface versus water in the assembly, the extent of the engineer&rsquo;s
            test openings, access, finish matching, and staging around residents.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Boundaries"
          title="What we do not do"
          lede="Several of these sit directly beside the work we perform and appear in the same reports, so they are worth establishing before anyone spends time on a bid."
        />

        <div class="grid-hairline cols-2">
          {boundaries.map((b) => (
            <div class="entry">
              <h3>{b.label}</h3>
              <p>{b.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/*
      FAQ. One entry carried an `href`/`hrefLabel` pair that the old hand-rolled
      <dl> rendered as a trailing link. FaqList takes {q, a} only, so rather
      than lose that route to the sibling page - or alter a shared component
      for one page - the same link and label render directly beneath the block.
      The destination and the label text are unchanged.
    */}
    <section class="section" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-head">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          id="faq-head"
          eyebrow="FAQ"
          title="Common questions from boards and managers"
        />
        <FaqList items={faqs} />

        {faqs.filter((f) => f.href).map((f) => (
          <p class="more">
            <a href={f.href} set:html={f.hrefLabel} />
          </p>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    {/*
      Commercial CTA. Not the shared <ContactCta /> — that is homeowner
      voiced, singular "I", and routes to the paid residential Moisture
      Check. It is not imported on this page. The brief specifies a separate
      CTA for this funnel: not "free estimate" but "send us the report."
      The brief also names a downloadable capability sheet. It does not
      exist and cannot be honestly assembled from null proof fields, so it
      is omitted rather than promised.
      Copy is unchanged from the pre-redesign CTA; only the band is new.
      PHONE stays null-gated sitewide. No number hardcoded.
    */}
    <section class="section on-dark" id="send">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="cta-commercial">
          <h2>Send us the engineer’s report</h2>
          <p>
            If you have a report, send it &mdash; along with the engineer&rsquo;s
            specification if it has been issued, and photographs of the elevations
            involved. We will come back with what the exterior wall scope in it
            involves, what we would need to see on the building to price it, and
            where we think the document is silent on something that will matter
            later.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the inspection is still ahead of you, tell us the building, its age,
            and what the walls look like now. That is enough to talk usefully about
            what the exterior wall portion tends to involve and what belongs in a
            budget or a reserve line before anyone is working to a deadline.
          </p>
          <p>
            Association and property-manager enquiries are handled directly
            rather than through the residential form.
          </p>
          {email && (
            <p class="cta-line">
              <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>
            </p>
          )}
          <p class="cta-line">
            <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>
          </p>
          <p class="cta-line">
            <a href="/commercial-stucco/condo-hoa-stucco-repair/"
              >Condo &amp; HOA stucco repair</a
            >
          </p>
          <p class="cta-line">
            <a href="/commercial-stucco/">All commercial &amp; condo work</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />

  <MobileStickyBar />
</Layout>

